2704. To Be Or Not To Be
Solved
Easy
premium lock icon
Companies
Write a function expect that helps developers test their code. It should take in any value val and return an object with the following two functions.

toBe(val) accepts another value and returns true if the two values === each other. If they are not equal, it should throw an error "Not Equal".
notToBe(val) accepts another value and returns true if the two values !== each other. If they are equal, it should throw an error "Equal".
 

Example 1:

Input: func = () => expect(5).toBe(5)
Output: {"value": true}
Explanation: 5 === 5 so this expression returns true.
Example 2:

Input: func = () => expect(5).toBe(null)
Output: {"error": "Not Equal"}
Explanation: 5 !== null so this expression throw the error "Not Equal".
Example 3:

Input: func = () => expect(5).notToBe(null)
Output: {"value": true}
Explanation: 5 !== null so this expression returns true.

var compose = function(functions) {
    return function(x) {
        let result = x;
        for (let i = functions.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            result = functions[i](result);
        }
        return result;
    };
};




1️⃣ Closures

What it is:
A closure is when an inner function “remembers” variables from its outer function, even after the outer function has finished executing.

In your code:

var expect = function(val) {
    return {
        toBe: function(expected) {
            if (val === expected) { return true; }
            throw "Not Equal";
        }
    }
}


val is defined in the outer function expect.

The inner function toBe can still access val, even after expect has returned.

This is a closure.

Example simplified:

function outer(x) {
    return function inner(y) {
        return x + y;
    };
}

let add5 = outer(5);
console.log(add5(3)); // 8


✅ Just like toBe remembers val.

2️⃣ Functions returning objects

expect(val) returns an object with methods toBe and notToBe.

This allows you to do:

expect(5).toBe(5);


Without returning an object, you wouldn’t be able to “chain” the next method.

Simpler example:

function createObj(x) {
    return {
        print: function() { console.log(x); }
    }
}

let obj = createObj(10);
obj.print(); // 10

3️⃣ Method chaining / Fluent API

Because expect(val) returns an object, you can immediately call a method on it.

This is called a fluent API (common in testing libraries like Jest).

expect(5).toBe(5);
expect(5).notToBe(3);


Think of it as: “Give me something to test, then call methods on it.”

4️⃣ Error handling with throw

Instead of returning false when a check fails, the code throws an error:

throw "Not Equal";


In real testing libraries, this stops the test and marks it as failed.

This is why:

expect(5).notToBe(5); // throws "Equal"


If the expectation passes, it returns true (or nothing, in real libraries).

5️⃣ Custom assertion logic (testing concept)

You are essentially building a mini testing library.

expect() + .toBe() is exactly like Jest or Mocha assertions:

expect(actualValue).toBe(expectedValue);
expect(actualValue).notToBe(expectedValue);


This pattern is called “assertion library”.

6️⃣ Putting it all together
expect(5).toBe(5);      // ✔ passes
expect(5).notToBe(5);   // ❌ throws error


expect(5) → returns an object (concept: function returns object)

.toBe(5) → uses closure to access 5 inside expect

.notToBe(5) → uses throw to indicate failure

Fluent syntax → method chaining / readable API

